#include <stdint.h>
#include <string>
#include "muParser.h"

extern "C" int LLVMFuzzerTestOneInput(const uint8_t *data, size_t size) {
// Create a string from the fuzzer's raw input.
// The fuzzer will mutate this string to explore different expressions.
std::string expression(reinterpret_cast<const char*>(data), size);

// The mu::Parser object that we will exercise.
mu::Parser parser;

// Set the expression. This might throw an exception if the expression is
// invalid, which is the desired behavior for the fuzzer to detect and learn.
parser.SetExpr(expression);

// Call the target function. This function internally parses the expression
// set by SetExpr and will throw an exception if the syntax is invalid.
// By not catching the exception, we allow the fuzzer to treat it as a crash
// and guide its input generation towards valid syntax.
parser.GetUsedVar();

return 0;
}
</fuzz-target>

<function-requirements>

<input>
project name: muparser
function signature: const varmap_type & mu::ParserBase::GetUsedVar()
</input>

<description>
The `GetUsedVar` function is a constant member function of the `ParserBase` class that returns a constant reference to a map of the used variables in the expression. The function first sets a flag to ignore undefined variables, then calls the `CreateRPN` function to parse the expression and identify the variables. After `CreateRPN` completes, the flag to ignore undefined variables is reset. This allows the function to identify all variables present in the expression string, even if they haven't been assigned a value. The function is designed to be called after an expression has been set via `SetExpr`.
</description>

<requirements>
<requirement>
The `SetExpr` method must be called to set an expression string before calling `GetUsedVar`. `GetUsedVar` internally calls `CreateRPN`, which relies on the expression string being present to parse and identify the variables used.
</requirement>
<requirement>
The expression string provided to `SetExpr` should be fuzzed. This string represents user-controlled input and is directly processed by the parser logic within `GetUsedVar`.
</requirement>
<requirement>
There is no need to define variables using `DefineVar` or a variable factory before calling `GetUsedVar`. The function is specifically designed to identify variables in an expression even if they are not yet defined, as it internally suppresses errors for undefined variables during parsing.
</requirement>
</requirements>

</function-requirements>

